WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:07.049
The Barden Bellas are back making music with
their mouths in an aca-xplosion of song, dance,

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:09.790
and wisecracks.
In the first movie, the Bellas went from being

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:15.040
underdogs to the cream of the a capella crop.
But how long can they stay at the top? Not

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.869
very, it turns out as they come a cropper
right at start of this movie and have to fight

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:21.250
back to prove their aca-credentials.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:27.449
Pitch Perfect 2 very much marches to the same
beatbox as the first movie, which was a huge

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.820
sleeper hit back in 2012.
The sequel sees relationships cemented between

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.699
Bellas old and new, and adds some aca-dork-able
new connections to the mix.

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:41.750
Having worked together before, there’s an
easy chemistry between the film’s stars,

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.830
which is exactly what’s needed to bring
their on-screen characters back to life in

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.670
this musical tale of growing up.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.470
Anna Kendrick’s on fine form as Beca, who’s
trying to juggle finding her feet outside

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.780
college with her a capella duties.
Rebel Wilson is once again the joker of the

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.400
pack, and puts her improv skills to good effect
as Fat Amy.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.689
Brittany Snow as Chloe, who’s done everything
to stay in the Bellas, Ester Dean as Cynthia

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:09.490
Rose and Alexis Knapp as Stacie all put in
nice turns, even if their characters don’t

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.120
get a ton of development in the script.
And as Lily, Hana Mae Lee continues to amuse

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.600
and disturb in equal measure with her totally
off-the-wall and out-of-nowhere statements

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.100
in the middle of conversations.
Oh, and Anna Camp gets to put Aubrey’s controlling

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.550
ways to somewhat different use than when we
when we last saw her.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.810
This time round, the boys mainly take a backseat,
though Skylar Astin’s Jesse and Adam DeVine’s

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.780
Bumper both get some aca-action.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.850
The Bellas get a transfusion of new blood
thanks to Chrissie Fit who plays Flo, a Latina

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.450
who punctures many a melodramatic moment from
her fellow singers with tales of her own extreme

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:48.200
life experiences.
And Hailee Steinfeld, who was stunning in

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.200
True Grit alongside Jeff Bridges, brings a
sweetness and innocent enthusiasm to the role

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.570
of Emily, a first-year student keen to follow
in her mother’s footsteps by joining the

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.229
Bellas.
Birgitte Hjort Sørensen, star of Danish political

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:07.060
drama Borgen, and YouTube star Flula Borg
are spot-on as leaders of the Bellas’ rivals,

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.280
Das Sound Machine, a ruthlessly efficient
18-strong German group of tall trash-talkers,

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.810
who are all slicked-back hair and monochrome
military-style leather and fishnet outfits!

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.500
Plus there are some fun cameos including Keegan-Michael
Key’s scene-stealingly good turn as a top

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.420
music producer who gets some eminently quotable
lines.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.690
Expect lots more totally inappropriate banter
between Elizabeth Banks and John Michael Higgins

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:37.620
who put their perfect comic timing on display
again as they reprise their roles as the riotously

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.500
non-PC a capella commentators, Gail and John.
Yes, the movie trades in stereotypes, but

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.110
it feels like it does it with a self-awareness,
an ironic eye-roll and a wink to the audience,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.500
though sometimes it does miss its mark.
Speaking of winks, I could definitely have

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.980
lived without Fat Amy’s ‘no means yes’
joke, which also featured in the movie’s

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:58.270
trailer.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:03.690
By the way, Pitch Perfect 2 also marks Elizabeth
Banks’s debut as a feature-film director

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.959
and she does a good job maintaining a nice
pace throughout.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:11.410
As for the music, there’s a shed-load of
awesome mash-ups arranged by the movie’s

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:17.360
music director Ed Boyer, with songs from pretty
much every decade from the 1940s to the 2010s,

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.780
from hip-hop to electro pop, country to R&amp;B,
and even a brand new track.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.730
I’m pretty sure they’re headed for another
mega-smash hit with the movie’s soundtrack,

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:31.190
which is insanely catchy and full of feel-goodness!
Just like in the first movie, we get another

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:35.830
superb riff-off scene as well as an a capella
championship that take the Bellas out of their

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:39.790
collegiate comfort zone.
As for the routines, they’ve certainly got

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.650
the moves going on again this time!
There’s lots of ingeniously designed numbers

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.950
by choreographer Aakomon Jones including ones
that take props and pyrotechnics to the max.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.650
Oh, and do stick around for the credits as
otherwise you’ll miss an aca-treat!

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:59.090
So, to sum up: Thanks to its cast, compositions
and choreography, Pitch Perfect 2 manages

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.760
to mostly hit the right notes for a satisfyingly
fun, if rather familiar, sequel.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.840
Expect feel-good musical comedy that puts
a smile on your face.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.099
Pitch Perfect 2 gets 3.5 out of 5 Flicks.

